✅ Strengths (things it seems to do correctly)
Game Recovery at Startup:

It loads all non-completed games from the database (status != 'completed').

It rebuilds Player instances with the correct stack amounts from the DB.

It restores game configuration parameters like smallBlind and bigBlind.

Player Stack Restoration:

It recreates players with their previous chip stacks, so stacks won't "reset" after a server crash.

Game Configuration Restoration:

Small blind and big blind values are correctly restored per game.

Post-Action Sync:

After a player takes an action (fold, call, raise, etc.), it syncs player stacks back to DB immediately.

This is very good because it minimizes risk of desync if server crashes randomly.

Clean Service Separation:

It keeps all recovery and sync logic inside a dedicated service (GameRecoveryService), which is a very clean architecture decision (easy to test and maintain).

⚠️ Risks / Issues / Weaknesses I See (needs attention)
Not syncing hand-level game state (side pots, community cards, etc.)

Right now, only stacks are being synced.

But in a real poker game, after a crash, if you don't also persist side pots, board cards, betting round ("Flop", "Turn", etc.), you can lose the exact game situation.

➔ Fix: Also persist at least these parts after each action:

communityCards

currentBet

pot

sidePots

currentRound (e.g., "Turn")

No locking or transactional safety yet

If two actions happen really fast (example: two users click at once), you could get a race condition between memory and DB.

➔ Fix for later (production): Wrap syncs inside transactions (e.g., Prisma $transaction).

What if the database save fails?

Right now, no retry logic if syncing a player stack fails due to DB issue.

➔ Fix for later (production): Add retry mechanisms or alerting if saving fails.

Dealer index / Turn order is not restored yet

The dealerIndex and whose turn it is aren’t stored in DB (based on your last schema and Cursor's comments).

➔ Risk: After recovery, turn order might be wrong, which breaks the hand badly.

➔ Fix: Save and restore:

dealerIndex

currentTurnPlayerId (who's up next)

Blind levels are static

This is fine for now (you just restore smallBlind and bigBlind) — but if you ever implement tournaments with increasing blinds over time, you'll need dynamic blind recovery.

➔ Not urgent, just something to know.

